      "fieldData": {
       "awb---faq-data": "<div data-rt-embed-type='true'><div data-rt-embed-type='true'><script> window.awbFAQ = {\"heading\": \"PMU Consent Questions, Answered\", \"items\": [{\"q\": \"What is a permanent makeup consent form?\", \"a\": \"A permanent makeup consent form is the document a client reads and signs before a cosmetic tattoo procedure such as microblading or lip blush. It explains the procedure and its risks, records the client's answers to the artist's questions, and holds the signature.\"}, {\"q\": \"How do I make a permanent makeup consent form for my studio?\", \"a\": \"Start from the wording your artist uses at consultation. Describe it and your procedures to Emergent's <a href='https://emergent.sh/ai-form-builder'>AI form builder</a>, which builds the form, a table of signed records, and the photo uploads. Clients get a PDF of the signed permanent makeup consent form by email. Change any part later by asking in plain English.\"}, {\"q\": \"Is there a free permanent makeup consent form template?\", \"a\": \"Several form builders and studios post free ones. Any permanent makeup consent form template still carries another studio's wording. Read it next to your artist's consultation notes and keep only what your artist says out loud. Emergent can build the form from those notes instead, and it is free to start.\"}, {\"q\": \"What forms does a studio need besides the permanent makeup consent form?\", \"a\": \"A medical history intake usually comes first, and an aftercare sheet the client signs before leaving comes after the session. Studios that post healed results also need a photo release. On Emergent each of these saves to a database the studio owns, with no cap on responses.\"}, {\"q\": \"What should a microblading consent form include?\", \"a\": \"The client's details and date of birth, answers about earlier brow work, the initialed risk statements, and the signature. Microblading is a hand-tool tattoo that places pigment in hair-like strokes. Add the pigment and its lot number after the session, and the form becomes the session record too.\"}, {\"q\": \"Does lip blushing need its own permanent makeup consent form?\", \"a\": \"It needs its own risk statements more than its own document. Your artist explains lip blush differently from brows, and a client booking lips should initial only the lip statements. A form that branches by procedure keeps one record per client.\"}, {\"q\": \"Is an ombre powder brows consent form different from microblading?\", \"a\": \"The structure is the same, from the screening questions to the signature. The technique field changes, along with any risk your artist explains differently for shading. One brow form with a technique choice keeps both services on the same client record.\"}, {\"q\": \"Is a semi permanent makeup consent form any different?\", \"a\": \"No, it is the same document under another name. Brow studios often call microblading semi-permanent because the pigment fades, yet the FDA still describes permanent makeup as a type of tattoo. Studios that also do larger body work can keep a separate tattoo consent form for it.\"}, {\"q\": \"Can I offer a microblading consent form in Spanish?\", \"a\": \"Yes. Ask Emergent for a Spanish version and the client picks a language on the first screen. Have a fluent speaker check the risk statements first, since a translated warning has to say exactly what the English one says. Both versions save to the same table.\"}, {\"q\": \"Does brow lamination need a permanent makeup consent form?\", \"a\": \"No. Lamination is not a tattoo, and the tattoo risk statements on a PMU form do not apply to it. Studios that offer lamination and tinting can collect those details with an esthetician intake form instead.\"}]}; </script></div></div>"
      }
